## Key Patterns & Conventions Used

### 1. RESTful Resource Naming
All endpoints follow the convention `/books` (plural noun) with HTTP methods determining the action:
- `GET /books` → read collection
- `POST /books` → create resource
- `GET /books/:id` → read single resource
- `PUT /books/:id` → full update
- `DELETE /books/:id` → delete

### 2. HTTP Status Codes
| Code | Meaning | Used When |
|------|---------|-----------|
| 200 | OK | Successful GET, PUT, DELETE |
| 201 | Created | Successful POST (new resource) |
| 400 | Bad Request | Invalid input / validation failure |
| 404 | Not Found | Resource doesn't exist |
| 500 | Internal Server Error | Unexpected server error |

### 3. Async/Await Note
This implementation uses synchronous handlers because the in-memory store operations are instant. In a real app with database calls, you would use `async/await`:

```javascript
// Example of how an async route would look:
router.get('/:id', async (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    const book = await Book.findById(req.params.id);
    if (!book) throw new NotFoundError(`Book ${req.params.id} not found`);
    res.json(book);
  } catch (err) {
    next(err); // Pass to global error handler
  }
});
```

### 4. Why `module.exports.getBooks = () => books`?
This exposes a read-only getter for the stats endpoint in `server.js` without allowing external code to mutate the array directly. It's a simple form of encapsulation.

